From b3288a5f1d29a01c6abb41fc1fa878eaa3812c85 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: khj1222 <bluekgssk@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 18:51:11 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] patch cache: notice when a label asset changed under it

The flat patch cache identifies a segment's labels by path. Pointing at a different label
tree is caught, because the cached paths no longer match any current segment. Regenerating
a mask in place is not: the paths still match, the patch-finding token is unchanged, and
the previous split is reused against labels it no longer describes.

Measured on a real segment: discover 1,266 patches, halve the supervision mask in place,
rerun in the same out_dir -> 1,266 patches again, byte-identical bounding boxes. The truth,
from a fresh out_dir, is 1,162. So 104 patches keep training on supervision that no longer
exists, and nothing says so.

Add a digest of each label asset's relative file names and sizes to the segment cache key
and to the cached records. Rewriting a zarr changes chunk sizes, and dropping annotation
deletes chunks outright when the store does not write empty ones, so the digest moves and
the cache is rejected. After the change the same reproduction returns 1,162 with the same
bounding boxes as the fresh run.

It costs one directory walk over the label assets -- os.scandir carries the size, so a
6,429-file label array fingerprints in 8 ms -- and the cache still hits: a second discovery
on an unchanged tree takes 0.02 s against 3.03 s for the first, and a byte-identical copy of
a tree fingerprints the same and hits too. It reads no chunk contents, so two different
labels that compress to identical sizes under identical names would not be distinguished.

61 tests pass across data, common and inference.
